[x] Make query output consistent across named/index and add-charter $Fixed: add action/add charter/delete action now emit VerbOutcome::Added/Deleted through verb_result::emit (JSON when piped, prose at a terminal), matching update/complete/cancel/reopen. Decided: CLI-only outcomes reuse the same VerbOutcome/transaction_result.schema.json rather than a separate schema (its own doc comment already claims full mirroring); added reopened/added/deleted to the schema's kind enum and already-open to VerbError, and closed the spec_conformance gap that quietly skipped Reopened. Investigated and left alone: query named (standard SPARQL Results JSON) vs query index (curated JSON-LD/NDJSON index contract) are deliberately different query families per specifications/query_output.md, not an inconsistency to unify.$ ^2026-08-23T15:07 %2026-09-17T20:54 #01a030aa-2e92-79c1-b4dc-a0ad2e058ff8
[x] Purge journaling text and fix link definitions in workspace spec $workspace.md's Mutation durability section described the exclusive OS lock + .pending journal protocol direct-delivery retired. Confirmed against clearhead-workspace-fs: no lock file, no journal exist in the current implementation. Rewrote the section to describe the real mechanism (EffectBatch + per-resource precondition, optimistic concurrency, additions-before-removals ordering) and fixed the archive section's matching 'journalled' claim. Also fixed the actual link-definitions bug: all reference-style definitions sat on one line so only the first parsed; split one per line and corrected the decisions/decision-28 targets to ../docs/DECISIONS.md (moved out of the platform root tonight). Also fixed four stale DECISIONS.md/SUBMODULES.md/WORKTREES.md links in platform's own README.md, found during the same sweep. specifications fe6881d, platform 8acb8b6.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 %2026-09-17T21:59 #01a0a42b-3bf3-7423-a374-fc63bfec6d48
[x] Route charter markdown writes through EffectBatch delivery $jot, close and update call atomic_write directly and skip the precondition revision check. Confirmed 2026-09-17: this is NOT missing machinery to build from scratch — EffectBatch::preconditions + ResourcePrecondition/ExpectedResource::Revision + validate_preconditions + DeliveryError::Conflict/ResourceConflict already exist and are already enforced for action mutations (clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs). Charter markdown writes just never route through that seam; bring them onto the same EffectBatch+precondition path actions already use, don't build a second mechanism.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 0f49f67: added `CharterDocument` plus `read_charter_document`/`write_charter_document` to clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs. The read captures the `.md` revision (ExpectedResource::Revision, or Missing for a not-yet-materialized charter) and the write delivers a single `Effect::Write` batch carrying that revision as its precondition through the same `deliver` → `validate_preconditions` path action mutations use — no second mechanism. Conflict reporting is still flattened to a string at that call site; that is 01a0b2c2, the next task. In clearhead-cli/src/commands/charter.rs: `jot` now derives both its base content and its created-vs-updated reporting from the document it actually read (std::fs::read_to_string and durability::atomic_write are gone from that verb); `update_charter` and `close_charter` read purely for the precondition and keep regenerating content from the loaded model, so their happy path is unchanged. `charter_document_location` mirrors `validate_source_path`: the path must be under `charters/` and end in `.md`. Tests added in the same file: round-trip write, creation of a missing document, stale-revision rejection (asserts the newer content survives), and the path guard. Verified end-to-end by building the CLI and running init → add charter → jot → update charter → close charter in a scratch workspace (`doctor` clean), plus the full pre-push gate. `add charter`'s two own atomic_write calls are deliberately left alone — different verb, not this action's scope.$ !3 ^2026-08-31T22:07 %2026-09-17T23:02 #01a05b5d-62be-73c0-8e70-154d78bd57a3
[x] Surface DeliveryError\:\:Conflict as a structured VerbError\:\:Conflict $Action mutations already carry a real precondition check (EffectBatch::preconditions, validate_preconditions in clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs) and already detect a stale-write race as DeliveryError::Conflict{conflict: ResourceConflict{path, expected, actual}}. But lib.rs:492 immediately flattens it via .to_string() into WorkspaceError::Actions(String), so every CLI verb loses the shape and an agent just sees an opaque error, not something it can branch on. Add VerbError::Conflict{path, expected, actual} (or similar) and thread the real ResourceConflict through instead of stringifying it. Found while designing the agent-surface MCP tools: multiple concurrent agents writing through capture/transact is exactly the scenario that turns this from theoretical into routine.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 2aea96a: the conflict now travels as data at every layer that carries it. clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/mod.rs adds WorkspaceError::Conflict(ResourceConflict), and validate_preconditions (clearhead-workspace-fs/src/lib.rs) returns it instead of flattening via .to_string(). clearhead-core/src/verb_result.rs adds VerbError::Conflict{path, expected, actual} plus VerbError::from_workspace_error(&WorkspaceError); the module doc's "conflict joins later" note is corrected, since the write path has had compare-and-swap since the EffectBatch seam landed. clearhead-cli/src/main.rs searches the anyhow cause chain for a WorkspaceError, because commands wrap adapter errors in context, and emits {"kind":"conflict","path":...,"expected":...,"actual":...} on piped stdout like the other kinds. ResourceRevision, ExpectedResource and ResourceConflict gained Display for both the workspace error message and human stderr output. Tests: serialization of the new kind; projection from both a changed revision and a deletion underneath the write; the cause-chain walk behind anyhow context; and the workspace-fs seam test tightened from a message match to matches!(WorkspaceError::Conflict(_)). Deliberately not covered end-to-end at the CLI: producing a conflict requires real concurrency, so there is no external reproduction — the seam and the boundary are unit-tested instead. Full pre-push gate passed.$ !3 ^2026-09-17T21:25 %2026-09-17T23:09 #01a0b2c2-f791-733e-b323-6b16f3e7d24a
[x] Let add action create a missing charter actions file $promoting agent-surface failed with has no associated actions file; had to touch next.actions by hand. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core CLI fix (add action's charter-actions-file-creation path), out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Not started.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core 4852414: the anchor is now derived, not demanded. clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/pathing.rs adds actions_anchor_for_document — the inverse of the loader's pairing rule (README.md → next.actions, otherwise stem ↔ stem) — re-exported through store/workspace mod. clearhead-cli/src/commands/action.rs resolve_acts_file (the only caller; add_action is its only user) falls back to it when the charter has no actions_file, so the location comes from the charter's document and never from its title. That exposed a second blocker: clearhead-workspace-fs validate_source_path canonicalized the target and so rejected a file that does not exist yet, giving "Invalid path"; it now keeps the exact previous canonical-file check for an existing target and, for a not-yet-created one, requires the deepest existing ancestor to be inside the charter tree. Delivery creates the file as part of the insert, so the first action and its anchor are a single atomic write. Tests: pathing unit tests for the inverse rule; a workspace-fs test that a missing in-tree anchor (and one in a not-yet-existing subdirectory) validates while an outside or non-.actions path is still rejected; two CLI integration tests (md-only charter, README.md collection charter). Verified end-to-end in scratch workspaces: both shapes create the right anchor and doctor stays clean. Other verbs (update/delete/complete/reopen) keep erroring on a charter with no actions file — they have nothing to act on, and add_action is resolve_acts_file's only caller.$ ^2026-09-14T17:09 %2026-09-17T23:13 <adopt-unified-root #01a0a266-01cf-73e3-acf2-a669be313387
[x] Stop deriving charter ids from titles in parse_charter $a charter md without frontmatter id gets a v5 id recomputed from its title on every load, so a retitle silently changes identity; the concept identity rules forbid live recompute. init sidesteps it via charter_frontmatter_id; loading should report the gap for doctor instead. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core parser/doctor change, out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Not started.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core dd20473. parse_charter no longer derives an id from the title: specifications/workspace.md's Concept Identity section forbids recomputing an identity from mutable content, and the section's own answer for a missing anchor (the one the root clause states explicitly and workspace identity already uses) is an ephemeral id minted per load that loading never persists. clearhead-core/src/workspace/charter.rs now does `fm.id.unwrap_or_else(Uuid::now_v7)` with the rule documented at the function and in a new test (an id-less document's id is not v5(title) and differs between parses; a declared id survives a retitle). The gap is reported by the loader: crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/assembly.rs adds the warning finding charter-document-without-id for every charter document that declares no id, naming the document (md_file, not its actions anchor) and pointing at the remedy — a minting write such as `clearhead update charter <it>`, or `clearhead init` for a new workspace. A charter whose sidecar records charter.id keeps adopting it (recorded truth beats a mint), and that variant's message points at the frontmatter. The root README keeps doctor's existing repair-aware root-readme-without-id, so no case is reported twice. Fixture fallout, all of it cases that pinned the forbidden derivation: three workspace snapshot fixtures (user-flat/charters/work.md, md-merge/charters/health.md, project-mixed/charters/health/README.md) now declare the ids their recorded .ron snapshots already expect, and the hand-written charters in seven archive_charter tests declare ids. Tests added: charter.rs (no title derivation, declared id survives retitle) and crates/clearhead-workspace-fs/tests/doctor.rs (the finding, both the no-sidecar and sidecar-carries-the-id variants). Observed on the real platform workspace: 8 findings — agent-surface and the seven someday/ charters declare no id — so doctor now exits 1 with 8 warnings there and each load prints them; declaring an id, or any minting write such as `clearhead update charter agent-surface`, clears it. NOT DONE, deliberately out of this action's scope: implicit_charter still seeds identity for a document-less charter with v5(name), which is likewise path-derived; it is a different case with its own documented rationale and tests, and the action scopes to parse_charter. Also not done: giving the model a way to distinguish a declared id from an ephemeral one downstream (so `show charter` can label it), which would need a new field on Charter/MarkdownCharter.$ ^2026-09-15T01:55 %2026-09-17T23:19 #01a0a447-8bcd-77b1-bda1-ef3201183590
[x] Write vdir displayname metadata from charter alias $collection dirs stay stable path keys like plans/next; ClearHead writes the vdir displayname file from the alias and refreshes it on rename, so calendar apps show readable names and vdirsyncer metasync carries them. Cross-workspace uniqueness stays a transport mapping, not a folder name. Decided 2026-09-15. DEFERRED 2026-09-18: this is a clearhead-core CLI feature (writing the vdir metasync file), out of scope for the platform+specifications-only overnight pass. Design decision already made; only implementation remains. Not started.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core c0e9542. New clearhead-workspace-fs/src/calendar.rs `write_collection_displaynames(workspace_root, external_plans) -> Result<usize>`: loads the workspace, takes each charter's alias as its collection's display name, and atomically writes `<plans_root>/<plans_dir>/displayname` only when the content differs; returns how many files it wrote or refreshed. It honors the effective plans root (internal `plans/`, or the external vdir when `plan_path` is configured) via NativeWorkspaceMounts::plans_root. Two deliberate limits, both documented at the function: a charter with no alias is skipped (the folder name is then the only name available), and only collections that already exist are named, so writing metadata never brings an empty collection into being. The constant COLLECTION_DISPLAYNAME_FILE is exported alongside it. Wired at the two points the action names: `clearhead sync calendar` (clearhead-cli/src/commands/service.rs) names the collections its sync materialized, called before the "already in sync" early return — that return is exactly the case where the vdir exists but was never named; and `clearhead update charter --alias` (clearhead-cli/src/commands/charter.rs) refreshes the name on a rename, so a rename changes the displayname and not the collection path. The helper is public so the LSP and the future MCP surface can adopt it. Tests: two workspace-fs tests (alias → displayname, idempotent second call, no-alias and un-materialized collections untouched, rename refresh with the path unchanged; and the external plans root case). Verified end to end with the built CLI: with an existing `plans/work/`, `sync calendar` writes displayname `work`, and `update charter work --alias labor` rewrites it to `labor` while the directory stays `work`. Note for future work: per-plan writes (`write_plan_file`) are covered indirectly through the sync-level call, and a rename performed outside the CLI (hand edit, LSP) would need the same refresh wired wherever that client writes.$ ^2026-09-15T01:24 %2026-09-17T23:22 #01a0a42b-3bd4-71c1-b922-39b88e9c310b
[x] Drop the retired clearhead-graphd path from config.json's additional_workspaces $graphd was retired; the stale entry made any command scanning all workspace_dirs() without --charter/--file (e.g. delete action) fail with Invalid path instead of just skipping it. Found and fixed while verifying the add/delete VerbOutcome change.$ ^2026-09-17T20:54 %2026-09-18T22:12 #01a0b2a6-b3d3-714d-bb2d-347bd4df4ba5
[x] Run the gate script instead of re-typing the gate $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. The runbook lists the full pre-push gate as inline commands to run before every push; clearhead-core/.githooks/pre-push is already exactly that gate, versioned and printing its own PASS/FAIL. Duplicating the definition cost real time: the sequence was hand-assembled five times, and the hand-built version had a bug (grep exiting 1 on no matches in a pipefail shell) that killed two complete gate runs before switching to capturing test output to a file and grepping the file. Two edits. (1) platform docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, and whatever template it is cut from, replaces the inline command list with one instruction to run the gate script, plus the single piece of shell craft that matters: send cargo test --workspace output to a file and grep the file, because the tail window truncates the failure list. (2) clearhead-core extracts scripts/gate.sh that .githooks/pre-push calls, so CI, a human, and an agent all invoke one definition instead of three. Acceptance: the runbook names one command, and that command is the same file the hook runs. RESOLVED 2026-09-18, clearhead-core bbfd87e and platform docs. clearhead-core/scripts/gate.sh is now the single definition of the gate: one line per step, failure lines only, exit 1 if any step failed, and a fast flag that skips the tests. It captures each step's output in a variable and filters that, so it does not have the pipefail grep bug and does not truncate the failure list. .githooks/pre-push now just execs it, so the hook, CI's checks, a human and an agent invoke one file. Both platform docs that carried the inline command list (docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md and the Gate section of docs/crate-merge-and-charter-identity.md) now say to run sh scripts/gate.sh. The new successor runbook names the same command. Not changed: ci.yml still lists the steps individually, deliberately, for per-step CI reporting, and uses locked flags, so it can drift from the script; consider having CI call the script.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 %2026-09-18T22:14 #01a0b585-68cb-7397-b200-02ffaa6201ef
[x] Make parse_charter return an optional-id document and inject the domain id $RESOLVED 2026-09-18 night, clearhead-core e4c7b6d. parse_charter returns a public CharterDocument (id: Option<Uuid>) and no longer mints Uuid::now_v7. CharterDocument::into_charter(fallback_id) is the document-to-Charter conversion; workspace assembly (WorkspaceAssemblyInput.charter_ids) and Workspace::from_parts take shell-supplied ids, minted per load by clearhead-workspace-fs (mounts.rs assemble_native + load_workspace_envelope). Sidecar adoption preserved: frontmatter id -> sidecar charter.id -> shell ephemeral. Pure-core allowlist shrunk 9->7 (removed workspace/charter.rs and workspace/store/load.rs now_v7). Gate green; doctor still reports the 8 known charter-document-without-id warnings. Tests: parse never mints an id, undeclared gets the supplied id, declared id wins over supplied. Fresh-context reviewer found no I1-I12 violation; three non-blocking notes: (1) CharterDocument name collides with workspace-fs existing CharterDocument, defer rename to step-2 merge; (2) action-only charters (no .md) still mint ids in core via allowlisted domain constructors, known ratchet item; (3) shell mints one id per .md even when declared, harmless.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 %2026-09-18T23:09 <01a0b5d0-da21-72bf-b418-0d2645335267 #01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9
[x] Refuse to archive a charter that declares no id $RESOLVED 2026-09-18 night, clearhead-core 5cd5056. archive charter now refuses (typed ArchiveCharterError::UndeclaredId) any charter whose document declares no id in frontmatter, pointing at `clearhead normalize`, so an ephemeral per-load id is never baked into archive file names or the crystallized sidecar (I12). Action-only charters (no .md) keep their deterministic name-derived id and are unaffected. Added integration test archive_refuses_a_charter_whose_document_declares_no_id (asserts failure + no archive dir written) and updated 7 inline test fixtures to declare ids. Documented the refusal in the `archive charter` clap help. Gate green.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T12:24 %2026-09-18T23:40 <01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9 #01a0b5fa-6ab7-7100-a71d-4e1388c2b30f
[x] Fix resource.rs module doc that still describes the retired workspace lock $Found 2026-09-18 via an agent-workspace explain_stale dump. clearhead-core/crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/resource.rs opens with module docs saying the native adapter must still hold its workspace lock across recovery, inventory, reads, preparation, precondition validation and durable commit. Direct-delivery retired the lock and the journal; the precondition revision compare-and-swap is the only concurrency guard left, and it cannot detect inventory phantoms (a resource added concurrently that Core never saw), which is a real residual gap worth stating honestly in that doc instead. Rewrite the module doc to match the shipped delivery model. Same drift class as the workspace-fs README fix in 91b3fc5. Check the other module docs in workspace/ for the same retired vocabulary: lock, recovery, journal, pending. RESOLVED 2026-09-21. Rewrote resource.rs lines 9-12 to describe the shipped model (no lock, no journal replay; read, prepare EffectBatch, validate per-resource precondition as compare-and-swap, deliver in additive order) and kept the inventory-phantoms caveat. Grepped workspace/ and filesystem/ for the same retired vocabulary (lock, recovery, journal) and found 7 more sites repeating the pre-direct-delivery phrasing, all fixed the same way: clearhead-core/src/workspace/archive_charter.rs:3 (also fixed a mismatched-backtick typo), selector.rs:1-5, transaction.rs:120 and :249, calendar/reconcile.rs:854, clearhead-cli/src/filesystem/mod.rs:480-481 (transact doc), filesystem/doctor.rs:116 (apply_doctor_repairs doc), and three inline comments in cli/action.rs (lines ~44, ~480, ~565, all 'under the lock' -> 'before delivery'). Did not touch actions/source.rs's RecoveryReport (parser-recovery, unrelated concept) or store/doctor.rs's PendingJournal residue detection (still-live crash-residue check, not a doc-only fix -- left for a real look, not blind removal). cargo check --workspace is clean after all edits.$ !4 ^2026-09-18T22:04 %2026-09-21T20:07 #01a0b80d-8c36-7498-8017-9680cbe05b95
[x] Attribute lens findings to the turn, not the whole file $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. Four dead wiki-links in .clearhead/charters/support.md (lines 10, 69 and 70; present since commit 88799f81 on 2026-08-23) were reported as must-fix on roughly seven consecutive turns. The run did not introduce them: the only edit to that file was an appended log line, which put the whole file into the turn's delta, which surfaced every finding in it. That is structural rather than unlucky, because ClearHead's capture loop always appends to a charter document, so every log entry in any repository can trigger it. Three fixes in the linter, cheapest first. (1) Attribute by hunk: diff the file against HEAD for the turn and report findings only on changed lines; that alone removes the whole class. (2) If that is too coarse, add a suppression ledger that survives turns and carries provenance -- finding id, path, line, reason, who acknowledged it, when -- so the next turn reports 'four acknowledged, zero new' rather than a fresh stop. (3) Scope rules the repository deliberately does not follow to non-test code: the rust-unwrap advisory fired repeatedly on test modules where unwrap is the established convention, which trains the reader to ignore advisories. Note: the target is the agent harness linter rather than a ClearHead repository, so this is about making the tool trustworthy; it is filed here because it surfaced through dogfooding and because the capture loop is what triggers it.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 %2026-09-22T10:50 #01a0b585-68da-7037-a9d5-42f1ee1628ce
[x] Add an append-description verb for action resolutions $Found 2026-09-18 while running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, whose ground rules require every action's resolution to be written into its own description before completing it. clearhead update action --description REPLACES the inline note, so all five completions that night needed a shell workaround: extract the existing description out of the .actions line by splitting on the dollar delimiters in Python, concatenate the new analysis, and pass the whole thing back. Five identical hacks, and the split is fragile for any description that itself contains a dollar sign. This charter already names the altitude -- 'Action description = the distilled per-action outcome' -- but the write verb for that altitude has no append form. Fix: an append mode (clearhead update action --append-description, or a resolve/note verb) that appends a labelled block and never asks the caller to reconstruct existing text. Acceptance: a completion can stamp its resolution in one CLI call, with a round-trip test proving the original note survives verbatim, including a note that contains a dollar sign. Touch points: clearhead-cli/src/argparser.rs and src/commands/action.rs. Cross-reference: the closed action 'Add a reopen path for completed actions' (01a030aa-2e64) named retroactive description stamping as one of its motivations, and reopen does address the frozen case. It does not address this one: with or without reopen, --description still replaces, so the caller still has to reconstruct the existing note by hand. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in clearhead-core e75c71a. Added mutually exclusive --append-description to update action. Core models replacement versus append explicitly, so the append is applied to the freshly re-read action rather than reconstructing stale text in the CLI; non-empty text is separated by one space. Unit and CLI round-trip tests prove an original note containing a literal dollar sign survives verbatim. Full workspace tests, strict Clippy, no-default builds, WASM dependency gate, and pure-core source gate pass.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T10:16 %2026-09-22T11:15 #01a0b585-68c4-775a-ab77-a9ae3c17d941
[x] Edit charter documents as text in update, close and jot, with the revision from the same read $Second child, and the fix for review finding 1 of the overnight run. update charter and close charter currently compute formatted from an earlier load and then capture the revision in a later read_charter_document, so a concurrent edit between them is clobbered. Derive the new text from the CharterDocument content itself so the revision and the content come from one read, as jot already does. Use surgical text edits of the frontmatter and state, not format_charter, which also silently drops defaults frontmatter. Verbs must not stamp an id as a side effect. Only creating a whole new document writes one. Add a test that a change landing between the read and the write yields a conflict for update and close, not just for a write after a read. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in clearhead-core ab897f0. update charter and close charter now read the CharterDocument once, derive the edited text from that same content, and deliver against that read revision. Core performs surgical source edits for alias, state, frontmatter title, and H1 title while preserving unmodeled frontmatter and all untouched text. Existing id-less documents remain id-less; only close creating a missing document persists an id. jot already used the correct same-read seam and remains unchanged. Tests cover preservation of defaults and id omission plus typed conflicts for both update and close when another write lands between read and delivery. Full workspace tests, strict Clippy, no-default builds, WASM dependency gate, and pure-core source gate pass.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 %2026-09-22T11:25 <01a0b5a4-ec3f-76ad-a63a-ca655a83364c #01a0b5b0-17c4-73d7-8fa1-e9e4aebda796
[x] Stop dropping newlines inside an action note $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md, after passing multi-paragraph resolutions through clearhead update action --description. Newlines inside an action note are dropped with NO replacement separator, so the last word of one paragraph is joined to the first word of the next. Minimal repro: `clearhead add action probe --description "first paragraph here.<blank line>second paragraph after a blank line"` stores exactly `\[ ] probe \$first paragraph here.second paragraph after a blank line\$ ...` -- one line, and the boundary is invisible unless you look for it. Every one of the five resolutions written that night therefore reads `...don't build a second mechanism.RESOLVED 2026-09-18, ...` in the stored record. The read path has the same semantics: a hand-written note that contains a literal newline parses (no error, no lint complaint) and the model's description joins the two physical lines the same way, so a round trip through read/serialize also loses the boundary. This is not the formatter's escaping -- escape_field passes newlines through; the newline is already gone before formatting, i.e. the note field is single-line by construction at both boundaries. Why it matters now: the runbook's mandated workflow is 'write the resolution into the action's own description before completing', so notes are getting long enough to need paragraphs, and every one of them silently loses its structure. Three options, in order of ambition. (1) Minimum honest fix: normalize a newline to a single space (or reject it loudly) at both boundaries instead of deleting it, so text never silently runs together. (2) Right fix if notes are meant to carry paragraphs, which the workflow implies: allow continuation lines in the grammar so a multi-paragraph note round-trips verbatim, and teach --description to preserve what it is given. (3) If single-line notes are deliberate and permanent, say so in the DSL spec and have every writer normalize visibly. Recommendation: (1) now as a correctness fix, (2) as the real answer, since 'the distilled per-action outcome' for a multi-hour task will keep needing more than one paragraph. Touch points: the note parse/format path in clearhead-core/src/workspace/actions/parser.rs, the CLI description arguments in clearhead-cli/src/argparser.rs, and clearhead-cli/src/commands/action.rs. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 across specifications b672355, tree-sitter-actions 32f7f6f, and clearhead-core cc1c835. The format contract now says description interior whitespace is data. Topiary preserves input-dependent line breaks and blank lines between description children while retaining canonical single spacing around links and field boundaries.

Core pins the corrected grammar and has parser plus CLI mutation round-trip tests proving `--description` stores and reloads a two-paragraph note verbatim. The grammar/spec formatter suite, full workspace tests, strict Clippy, no-default builds, WASM dependency gate, and pure-core source gate pass.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T10:18 %2026-09-22T11:37 #01a0b586-aa5f-7796-aa3c-e9381eae7175
[x] Write compact language-server views for agents (outline, references, definition) $Decision 41. Raw nvim MCP language-server output is unusable at scale: a document outline of clearhead-workspace-fs lib.rs was about 20KB of JSON, larger than the file. Tested 2026-09-18: an exec_lua formatter returned the same outline as about 1.9KB (kind, name, 1-based line), a 10x cut. Build a small Lua module (suggest scripts/agent-nvim/compact.lua) with three views: outline (top level plus one level of children, no fields), references grouped by file with counts and 1-based lines, and definition as file and line. Agents call it through exec_lua. Note LSP lines are zero-based; the views must emit 1-based lines so they feed straight into a ranged read. Test the references view on MarkdownCharter id (about 45 sites across 11 files) and the definition view on write_charter_document; the definition view is untested so far. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in platform 8294aec. Added scripts/agent-nvim/compact.lua with read-only outline, references, and definition views over Neovim LSP requests; every emitted and accepted line is 1-based. Outline retains only kind/name/line and one child level, references group sorted lines and counts by file, and definition returns sorted file/line targets. README gives exact exec_lua usage and a headless-Neovim mock suite covers response shaping. Live rust-analyzer checks succeeded: the charter.rs outline was compact, MarkdownCharter references grouped 112 sites across 19 files, and write_charter_document resolved to filesystem/mod.rs:371. No LSP edit or rename surface is exposed.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T12:29 %2026-09-22T11:44 #01a0b5ff-3cd7-72b5-9e5c-9857bc0b19b3
[x] Add an agent-nvim launcher\: one shared read-only headless instance per worktree $Decision 41. Script (suggest scripts/agent-nvim with start, stop and status) that runs nvim headless with the users config on a socket keyed by worktree path, opens a Rust file so rust-analyzer attaches, and reports the connection target for the nvim MCP. One instance per worktree, shared by all agents there, never the humans editor. Read-only by policy: queries only, no lsp_apply_edit or rename. Measured 2026-09-18: rust-analyzer about 2GB plus nvim about 180MB, so stop must be easy and status should show memory. The LSP client name is rust_analyzer with an underscore; passing a hyphenated name makes wait_for_lsp_ready hang until it times out. Also finish the experiment: time warm queries, which was not measured. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in platform 00a1689. Added scripts/agent-nvim/server with start, status, target, and stop verbs. It derives a stable socket key from the canonical Git worktree path, starts one detached headless Neovim with the user config and a Rust buffer, waits for the rust_analyzer client, marks the dedicated instance read-only by policy, and reports its MCP target. Status reports the PID, rust-analyzer count, and aggregate RSS for Neovim plus all descendant language servers; stop performs a graceful remote quit with a termination fallback. README documents the queries-only policy and exact workflow. The isolated real-Neovim lifecycle test covers start, idempotent start, deterministic target, status, process-tree memory, and stop. Shell syntax, lifecycle, compact-view tests, and diff checks pass. Live platform measurement: five warm outline requests took 6-8 ms each; the configured editor and LSP process tree used about 1.16 GiB.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T12:29 %2026-09-22T12:04 <01a0b5ff-3cd7-72b5-9e5c-9857bc0b19b3 #01a0b5ff-3ce5-737e-b63f-7d736455a8da
[x] Add an independent review step to unattended runs $Found 2026-09-18. The overnight run passed every gate and still shipped a real compare-and-swap gap (update and close charter captured their revision after computing the new text); tests covered the mechanism, not the invariant. Add to the runbook template a review step before each submodule bump: a fresh agent, ideally a different model, reviews the diff against the invariants in the plan and reports findings, and a finding blocks the bump. Also add invariant-level tests where a mechanism test would not catch the break: no read verb writes, no write verb stamps an id, a change between read and write is a conflict for every charter verb. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 across clearhead-core 2e2ad07 and platform docs. The review is now a gate, not a suggestion: both overnight runbooks require a fresh agent (a different model when one is available) to review the task diff against the plan invariants before the platform submodule bump, with a finding blocking the bump; a same-model fresh context is named as a weaker fallback, never the review. The mechanical floor is now real tests, not prose. clearhead-core/crates/clearhead-cli/tests/charter_invariants.rs adds the invariant-level guards a mechanism test cannot substitute for: read_verbs_leave_the_workspace_bytes_untouched snapshots every workspace byte and runs show charter, show action, read charters/actions JSON, orient, query index, doctor and lint file, then asserts byte-for-byte equality (it also rejects clap usage exits so a typo cannot make it vacuous); jot_does_not_stamp_an_id_on_an_existing_charter covers I5 for the one write verb that lacked it. The delivery-seam test is renamed every_charter_write_verb_rejects_a_change_landing_between_its_read_and_write and now derives each verb text through its own production function, adding jot (append_log_entry) to update and close for I6. Platform docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md gains the review ground rule, docs/overnight-worker-crate-merge-and-identity.md now gates the bump on the review (it previously deferred the independent review to the morning brief), and docs/log.md records the change. Full clearhead-core gate passes: fmt, strict Clippy, workspace tests, no-default-feature builds, oxigraph leak check, WASM dependency gate, pure-core source gate.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 %2026-09-22T12:57 #01a0b5d0-da56-771e-afe7-12a0d08720b7
[x] Add the navigation and batching rules to the runbook template $Decision 41 and the analysis of the overnight export. Add to the runbook template and the overnight runbook: navigate code with the shared read-only language server and its compact views, grep only for text; batch independent tool calls in one turn (287 of 320 overnight turns made a single call); checkpoint per task, not once at the end. The plan doc crate-merge-and-charter-identity already carries the first two lines in its working protocol. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in platform docs. Both runbooks now carry the Decision 41 navigation and batching rule, and the successor no longer claims the helper is unbuilt. The rule names the concrete tools that now exist rather than the intent: navigate structural questions with the shared read-only Neovim (scripts/agent-nvim/server, platform 00a1689) and its compact views (scripts/agent-nvim/compact.lua, platform 8294aec) via exec_lua, grep only for text, and batch independent tool calls in one turn. It carries the measured evidence from the overnight export (287 of 320 turns made a single call) and keeps the read-narrowly limit (no whole-file reads over about 200 lines; use rg -n, a ranged read, or a compact view). Placement: docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md gains it as a ground rule, so runs cut from that template inherit it; docs/overnight-worker-crate-merge-and-identity.md replaces its Batch and read narrowly bullet, whose parenthetical said the language-server helper was not built. Per-task checkpointing was already stated in both runbooks, so only the navigation and batching half needed adding. The plan doc crate-merge-and-charter-identity.md already carried the first two lines in its Working protocol, unchanged; docs/log.md records the change.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T12:29 %2026-09-22T12:58 <01a0b5ff-3ce5-737e-b63f-7d736455a8da #01a0b5ff-3cf2-76e1-83b1-7f0aae63fc70
[x] Adopt a single-source rule for where each kind of knowledge lives $Found 2026-09-18: one decision was written into an action, a charter log, DECISIONS.md, a runbook and a memory file, which is how drift starts, and Decision 38 already cites a gate that does not exist. Write the rule into docs/CONTRIBUTING.md: decision text lives only in DECISIONS.md; actions hold task state and link to the decision; charter logs hold dated findings, not decisions; memory holds only what is not derivable from the repo; agent-workspace holds cited beliefs. Add the promotion path the agent-workspace versus clearhead boundary decision asks for: a finding worth keeping is promoted from the workspace to a clearhead jot or action on purpose, and nothing else copies it. Optionally add a doctor or okf lint that flags a decision heading in an action description. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in platform docs. docs/CONTRIBUTING.md gains a "Where knowledge lives" section stating the single-source rule with one home per kind of knowledge (architecture decisions in DECISIONS.md; task state in ClearHead actions; dated findings in a charter ## Log; durable repo facts in docs/; agent working memory in the agent workspace, which is not project truth) and spells out the consequences: an action links to a decision rather than restating it, a log line names the decision and the commit in one line, and memory holds only what is not derivable from the repo. It also documents the promotion path the agent-surface charter asks for: the agent workspace and ClearHead never sync, so a belief becomes project knowledge only by an explicit promotion to a clearhead jot (dated finding) or clearhead add action (task state), citing the belief id so the promotion is auditable, with the reverse link already provided by a claim external_reference. docs/log.md records it. The optional lint that would flag a decision heading pasted into an action description was deliberately deferred, not dropped: it needs a new lint code documented in specifications/linting.md and clearhead.nvim doc/clearhead.txt, plus a false-positive review, so it is filed as its own action 01a0cab3-ea83-7980-9b89-80bf3f92b90e with the narrow-match scope and the finding that no existing .actions file matches it today (it would ship quiet). The action original premise that Decision 38 cites a gate that does not exist was already fixed by 01a0b5d0-da21 (pure-core source gate).$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:39 %2026-09-22T13:00 #01a0b5d0-da38-74c4-bd29-ab0ecda10946
[x] Give the run report levels distinct jobs and lead with the blocker $Found 2026-09-18 running docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md. The runbook requires the report to live in the work rather than in chat, which is right, but it produced the same facts four times: a long action description, a charter log line, a per-task checkpoint note, and a final checkpoint -- all prose summaries of one thing. Cost: writing and reading the same analysis three extra times, with drift risk between the copies. Three changes, all to the runbook and its task template. (1) Distinct roles, not copies: the action description is the full authoritative analysis; the charter log line is one line naming the decision and the commit, never a recap; the per-task checkpoint is machine-shaped (task id, commit, status); the final checkpoint is an index of those, not a narrative. (2) A stopped task's description must LEAD with the decision it needs -- a NEEDS DECISION line naming the question -- then the analysis. Two tasks stopped that night with the analysis appended after the original context, so the first thing a reader meets is the old description, not the blocker. (3) Findings outside the run's scope must still be filed as actions in the owning charter. The rule against unilaterally resolving docs-versus-log source-of-truth is right, but the run had no channel for an out-of-bounds finding, so a real one (four dead wiki-links in this charter) would have ended up only in agent-local scratchpad memory, where the project cannot see it. Acceptance: the next run's artifacts hold one full analysis, one-line logs, machine-shaped checkpoints, stopped tasks whose first line is the question, and zero findings that exist only in chat or agent memory. Note: this edits the same file as the separately-owned premise-field change, so land them together. RESOLVED 2026-09-22 in platform docs. Both overnight runbooks now give the run report distinct levels instead of four prose copies of one analysis, and both carry the other two rules the action named. The rule states one job per artifact: the action description is the full authoritative analysis (what was found, decided and changed, with commits); the charter log line is one line naming the decision and the commit, never a recap; the per-task checkpoint is machine-shaped as {task id, commit, status} rather than prose; the final checkpoint is an index of those task checkpoints, not a narrative. Cost stated in the rule: writing one analysis at four altitudes costs three extra reads and invites drift. Stopped-task handling is now explicit in both runbooks: the action description must LEAD with a NEEDS DECISION line naming the question, followed by the analysis and the options, then the worker moves on -- not append the analysis after the old context, which is the failure mode the action recorded. Out-of-bounds findings must be filed as actions in the owning charter with the evidence (clearhead add action) rather than living only in chat or agent-local memory. Placement follows the runbook split: docs/overnight-worker-clearhead-core.md carries all three as ground rules so runs cut from that template inherit them; docs/overnight-worker-crate-merge-and-identity.md adds the report-levels and out-of-bounds-filing rules beside its existing checkpoint-per-task and Stop on a NEEDS DECISION bullets, which already required leading with the question. docs/log.md records the change. Per-task checkpointing already existed in both runbooks, so only its shape needed specifying. The action note about landing with the separately-owned premise-field change did not bind here: that change (the charter-map action 01a0a266-01c4) is still blocked and touches the charter README maps, not these runbooks.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T10:16 %2026-09-22T13:02 #01a0b585-68d2-721c-a2da-c07fc0353c30
[x] Stamp missing charter ids in normalize and point the doctor lint at it $Resolved 2026-09-22 (core fb5fd5f, night/2026-09-22): normalize file now stamps missing charter document frontmatter ids without reformatting other metadata or prose (crates/clearhead-cli/src/cli/file.rs). It adopts an existing sidecar charter id, preserves CRLF/mixed line endings and null-id comments, refuses ambiguous YAML keys without writing, and reparses before write. Doctor warnings for root and non-root documents now point to normalize file <path> --write (crates/clearhead-core/src/workspace/store/{assembly,doctor}.rs); behavior and non-stamping existing update/close/jot are documented in crates/clearhead-cli/{README.md,docs/UI.md}. Integration tests cover idempotence, sidecar adoption, doctor, malformed cases in tests/{normalize,fs_doctor}.rs. Also fixed an unrelated LSP stdio test race by consuming didSave diagnostics before the next change (tests/lsp_stdio_protocol.rs); its test passed 20 consecutive runs. Full sh scripts/gate.sh passed after the fix; fresh read-only independent reviewer found no remaining issues.$ !3 ^2026-09-18T11:03 %2026-09-22T22:50 <01a0b5b0-17b8-77e8-82e9-971c194e64f9 #01a0b5b0-17d2-717c-938d-74ae3d848ce1
[x] Split charter document identity from domain identity $DECIDED 2026-09-18 with the user. The file-level charter document has an OPTIONAL id, since a person may not have written it yet. The domain Charter REQUIRES one. Strictness increases at the conversion into the domain type. That conversion takes the id as an argument supplied by the shell (as Workspace from_parts already does for ephemeral_id), so clearhead-core stays pure: no clock or RNG inside parse_charter or assembly, which reverses the Uuid now_v7 added in dd20473. Resolution order: frontmatter id, then sidecar charter id, then the shell-supplied ephemeral id, never persisted by a read. Only normalize stamps a missing id, as the deliberate you-may-add-stuff pass, matching actions. Creation of a whole new document (jot on a missing file, close on an implicit charter) also writes the id, as add does for actions. No other write verb stamps as a side effect. Write verbs need no id at all: they edit document text and never round-trip through the domain Charter. Known trade-off: an id-less charter has a different identity each load, which only affects identity consumers such as query IRIs, and the charter-document-without-id lint plus doctor keep it visible. Three ordered children below. Origin: review of the overnight run, findings 1 to 3. RESOLVED 2026-09-22: all three ordered children landed: optional-id document and shell-supplied domain identity (core e4c7b6d), same-read document-text charter edits (core ab897f0), and explicit normalize minting with doctor guidance (core fb5fd5f, platform 0605d6f). Full core gate and independent review passed for the last child; its night branches were pushed.$ !2 ^2026-09-18T11:03 %2026-09-22T22:52 #01a0b5b0-17a8-73c8-878a-cdc69737e59d
[x] Add a lint for decision text inside an action description $Follow-up from the single-source knowledge rule (support 01a0b5d0-da38), found 2026-09-22. The rule now says decisions live only in DECISIONS.md and actions link to them, but nothing enforces it: a decision heading pasted into an action description is still invisible. Add a lint that flags it. Decided scope when the rule was written: a narrow rule, not a broad prose detector. Match a genuine decision heading only -- a markdown heading or a Decision <n>: label inside the description -- NOT the legitimate inline citations the repo already uses (DECIDED 2026-09-18, Decision 41, RESOLVED ...), which are links and task history, not duplicated decision text. Verified 2026-09-22 that no existing .actions file matches the narrow pattern, so the rule ships quiet. Deferred rather than done with the rule because it needs a new lint code, which means documenting it in specifications/linting.md and clearhead.nvim doc/clearhead.txt in the same commit, plus a false-positive review of historical action notes. Touch points: clearhead-core/src/workspace/actions/lint.rs (a new check beside check_excessive_duration), the lint code registry in specifications/linting.md, and the diagnostic list in clearhead.nvim doc/clearhead.txt. RESOLVED 2026-09-22: W014 warns only on a decision-labelled Markdown heading or line-start Decision N: inside an action description; inline Decision 41, DECIDED dates, RESOLVED notes and fenced examples remain quiet. Core c843cfc registers the rule and tests positives and false-positive history; specifications 97167d3 and clearhead.nvim d0da3bb document the code. No existing .actions file matched the narrow pattern. Full core gate and Neovim Busted suite pass; fresh different-model reviewer reports no blocking findings. Known non-blocker: the action parser normalizes description indentation, so this action-level check cannot distinguish a four-space-indented fence from a real fence without a future 